Bronze Design

Each line separated by semicolons (;).
Each line is a "command" separated by
spaces.

Examples:
-log (hello);
greet Gary int:34;

The first value represents the 
name of that command followed by
arguments. A dash (-) before the 
command indicates a low-level 
command, which is programmed into 
the interpreter. Otherwise, it is 
a Bronze command, which runs Bronze 
code. When the interpreter sees a 
Bronze command, it will look for 
a Bronze file (bze) of the same 
name and execute the code inside.

Any command file (a Bronze file containing 
code for a Bronze command) should have headers
indicating parameters.

Example in greet.bze:

PARAMS name=str age=int;
-log (Hello, );
-log ref:name;
-log ( of age );
-log ref:age;

Which should print "Hello, Gary of age 34"
upon being called from the above example.

The parentheses tell the interpreter not
to interpret the spaces inside of them
as delimiters for the arguments of a command.
Parentheses should be used when giving a
string containing spaces as a single parameter.

Colons in a parameter tell the interpreter
what kind of value is being given.

Some examples of parameters with types are:
int:8, ref:variable, float:1.2

The bit before the colon is the name of the
type. "int" represents integers, "ref" represents
variable values, "float" represents floats and
so on.

If there is no type declaration, the parameter
will be interpreted as a string by default.


"Bare" bronze will only include low-level
commands for reading and writing to files,
handling raw binary data, controlling the
interpreter and adding output to the terminal. 
Any other functionality like variables and 
functions will be implemented with bronze 
commands using low-level commands.


The intepreter should ignore newlines,
redundant spaces and text between graves (`) (comments)


The memory and values relating to the Bronze
program will not be stored as their respective
datatypes in the programming language in which
the interpreter is written, but as integers
or raw binary in a heap.